This product includes software developed as part of the AI App Factory project
(https://github.com/katayama502/ai-app-factory).

This distribution bundles or depends on third-party open-source software. No tagged release
exists yet (see ROADMAP.md), so there is no `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` to publish yet either — a
full, machine-generated attribution list (via `license-checker`) is planned to accompany the
first real release, once one exists. `pnpm sbom` (see README) already generates a machine-readable
inventory of every currently-resolved npm dependency with its license, which is a reasonable
substitute until then.

Actually integrated and running today (see README's "Try it today"):

  - Keycloak (Apache-2.0)      — Identity Provider
  - PostgreSQL (PostgreSQL License) + pgvector (PostgreSQL License)
  - Valkey (BSD-3-Clause)      — planned queue/cache; provisioned in `deploy/compose/` but nothing
                                   uses it yet

Planned per the design docs, NOT yet integrated into this codebase (listed here so their license
compatibility is already on record before they're added, not as a claim that they're bundled now):

  - Gitea (MIT)                 — Spec version history (M6, ADR-009)
  - Traefik (MIT)                — Reverse proxy / TLS termination
  - Zot (Apache-2.0)             — OCI registry
  - Ollama (MIT)                 — Local LLM runtime
  - Opengrep (LGPL-2.1)           — SAST, to be invoked as a separate process, not linked
  - OSV.dev (code: Apache-2.0, data: CC-BY-4.0) — Vulnerability database
  - OPA (Apache-2.0)              — Policy engine

gitleaks (MIT) and Trivy (Apache-2.0) already run today, but as CI Actions
(`.github/workflows/security.yml`) that scan this repository — not as software bundled into the
shipped product, which is what this NOTICE file itself covers.

See 03_REPOSITORY_リポジトリ構成とOSS運営.md §2.3 and §3.4 for the license-compatibility
rationale. A CI-enforced license denylist (AGPL / SSPL / Sustainable Use License / BSL) is
planned but not built yet — see the comment at the bottom of `security.yml`.
